  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Isopropyl alcohol may interact with Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase through a series of target relationships. Specifically, Isopropyl alcohol is linked to Tumor necrosis factor receptor superfamily member 10B, which in turn is a target of multiple compounds, including 4-Ethyl-3-Methyl-N-[2-[4-[(4-Methylcyclohexyl)Carbamoylsulfamoyl]Phenyl]Ethyl]-5-Oxo-2H-Pyrrole-1-Carboxamide and (+)-Verticillol. These compounds are all predicted to target Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, indicating a potential pathway through which Isopropyl alcohol may influence this enzyme. The scores from the paths suggest a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains unclear. The evidence highlights the possibility of a broader network of interactions involving Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, but further research is needed to elucidate the precise role of Isopropyl alcohol in this context.",
